firstLabel returns the first label or null for empty input. Its return inside the loop triggers the generator’s cleanup before the function’s promise completes.
```javascript
async function* asyncLabels(labels, events) {
  try {
    for (const label of labels) yield await Promise.resolve(label);
  } finally {
    events.push("closed");
  }
}
async function firstLabel(labels, events) {
  for await (const label of asyncLabels(labels, events)) return label;
  return null;
}
const events = [];
console.log(await firstLabel(["read", "draw"], events));
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output: read then closed.

This direct call shows Symbol.asyncIterator returning the same async-generator iterator, next returning a Promise for a record, and return requesting early completion. Awaiting return runs finally before inspection.
```javascript
async function* asyncLabels(labels, events) {
  try {
    for (const label of labels) yield await Promise.resolve(label);
  } finally {
    events.push("closed");
  }
}
async function firstLabel(labels, events) {
  for await (const label of asyncLabels(labels, events)) return label;
  return null;
}
const events = [];
const iterator = asyncLabels(["one", "two"], events);
console.log(iterator[Symbol.asyncIterator]() === iterator);
console.log((await iterator.next()).value);
console.log((await iterator.return()).done);
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output: true, one, true, closed. Optional practice: consume all labels with for await...of and explain why cleanup still runs.

