This example takes only the first label.
```javascript
async function* asyncLabels(labels, events) {
  try {
    for (const label of labels) yield await Promise.resolve(label);
  } finally {
    events.push("closed");
  }
}
async function firstLabel(labels, events) {
  for await (const label of asyncLabels(labels, events)) return label;
  return null;
}
const events = [];
console.log(await firstLabel(["read", "draw"], events));
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output:
```text
read
closed
```
The return inside the loop gives back read. Before `firstLabel` finishes, the generator's finally block records closed. Empty input gives `null` instead.

You can also ask an async iterator for values directly.
```javascript
async function* asyncLabels(labels, events) {
  try {
    for (const label of labels) yield await Promise.resolve(label);
  } finally {
    events.push("closed");
  }
}
async function firstLabel(labels, events) {
  for await (const label of asyncLabels(labels, events)) return label;
  return null;
}
const events = [];
const iterator = asyncLabels(["one", "two"], events);
console.log(iterator[Symbol.asyncIterator]() === iterator);
console.log((await iterator.next()).value);
console.log((await iterator.return()).done);
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output:
```text
true
one
true
closed
```
`Symbol.asyncIterator` returns this generator's iterator. `next()` requests a value, and `return()` asks it to finish early. Both are awaited.

Complete `firstLabel` using `for await...of`. Keep the supplied generator unchanged. An empty string is still a valid first label.
